No. Point-finiteness does **not** imply local finiteness, even when the space is regular (indeed, even when it is metrizable). Below is an explicit counterexample on $X=\mathbb{R}$.

---

### Counterexample

Let $X=\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology. $\mathbb{R}$ is metrizable, hence regular (in fact normal). Define an open cover indexed by $S=\{0\}\cup\mathbb{N}_{\ge 1}$ as follows:

- $A_0=\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}$,
- $B=(-1,1)$ (indexed separately, call it $A_\ast$),
- for each $n\ge 1$, $\displaystyle A_n=\Bigl(\tfrac{1}{n+1},\tfrac{1}{n}\Bigr)\cup\Bigl(-\tfrac{1}{n},-\tfrac{1}{n+1}\Bigr)$.

(To keep notation light I write the family as $\{A_0,\,A_\ast\}\cup\{A_n:n\ge 1\}$.)

**It is an open cover.** Every set is open. For coverage:
- $0\in A_\ast$.
- If $0<|x|<1$, then $x\in A_0$ (since $x\ne 0$) and also $x\in A_\ast$.
- If $|x|\ge 1$, then $x\in A_0$.
- If $x=\pm\tfrac{1}{n}$ for some $n\ge 1$, then $x\in A_0$ and $x\in A_\ast$ (and $x\notin$ any $A_m$, since each $A_m$ is a union of open intervals excluding its endpoints).

Hence every point of $\mathbb{R}$ is covered.

**It is point-finite.** Check each type of point:
- $x=0$: belongs only to $A_\ast$ (not to $A_0$, and not to any $A_n$). — $1$ set.
- $x=\pm\tfrac{1}{n}$: belongs to $A_0$ and $A_\ast$, and to no $A_m$ (endpoints are excluded). — $2$ sets.
- $0<|x|<1$ with $x\ne\pm\tfrac{1}{n}$: belongs to $A_0$, $A_\ast$, and to exactly one $A_m$ (the unique $m$ with $\tfrac{1}{m+1}<|x|<\tfrac{1}{m}$). — $3$ sets.
- $|x|\ge 1$: belongs only to $A_0$. — $1$ set.

So every point lies in at most $3$ members of the cover; in particular the cover is point-finite.

**It is NOT locally finite.** Consider the point $x=0$. Let $U$ be any neighborhood of $0$; then $U$ contains some $(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)$ with $\varepsilon>0$. Choose $N$ with $\tfrac{1}{N}<\varepsilon$. For every $n\ge N$ we have $\tfrac{1}{n}\le\tfrac{1}{N}<\varepsilon$, so the interval $(\tfrac{1}{n+1},\tfrac{1}{n})\subset(-\varepsilon,\varepsilon)\subset U$ is nonempty and lies inside $A_n$. Thus $U\cap A_n\neq\emptyset$ for **all** $n\ge N$, i.e. for infinitely many indices. Therefore no neighborhood of $0$ meets only finitely many members of the cover, so the family is not locally finite at $0$.

---

### Conclusion

The hypothesis "every point is contained in only finitely many $A_i$" (point-finiteness) is strictly weaker than local finiteness. The open cover above of the regular (metrizable) space $\mathbb{R}$ is point-finite yet fails to be locally finite. Hence we **cannot** deduce local finiteness from point-finiteness, even under the assumption that $X$ is regular.

